13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 2–14, 1864","place":"West Virginia and Cloyd’s Mountain near Dublin, Virginia","result":"Crook defeated Jenkins at Cloyd’s Mountain, captured the Confederate commander, burned the New River railroad bridge, and damaged the Virginia and Tennessee Railroad. He then withdrew to West Virginia, ending the sustained threat that Grant intended","facts":[{"label":"U.S. force at Cloyd’s Mountain","value":"About 6,500"},{"label":"Confederate force at the battle","value":"About 3,000"},{"label":"Cloyd’s Mountain elevation","value":"About 2,600 feet"},{"label":"Confederate guns observed","value":"9"},{"label":"U.S. casualties","value":"688","note":"About 10 percent of the force"},{"label":"Confederate casualties","value":"538","note":"About 23 percent of the force"},{"label":"New River railroad bridge","value":"About 400 feet long"},{"label":"Distance from New River to Christiansburg","value":"About 12 miles"}],"maps":[{"title":"Attack at Cloyd’s Mountain","caption":"The schematic shows Jenkins’s fence-rail breastworks on Shuffle Ridge, Back Creek in front of the position, Crook’s two-brigade frontal attack, and Carr White’s wider movement against the Confederate right.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va049","image":"/images/cloyds-mountain-tactical-map.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical map of the Battle of Cloyd’s Mountain on May 9, 1864"},{"title":"Cloyd’s Mountain, Dublin, and the New River today","caption":"The battlefield lay north of Dublin along the route from Pearisburg. After the battle, Crook moved through Dublin to the Virginia and Tennessee Railroad bridge over the New River.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-81.05%2C36.85%2C-80.45%2C37.35&layer=mapnik&marker=37.1126%2C-80.6654"},{"title":"The western Virginia expedition","caption":"Crook marched from the Kanawha Valley across the Allegheny Mountains toward Dublin while Averell was assigned targets at Wytheville and Saltville. The combined operations covered a large part of southwestern Virginia.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-82.3%2C36.3%2C-79.7%2C38.4&layer=mapnik&marker=37.1126%2C-80.6654"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:07","audioTime":67,"title":"Grant coordinated multiple Virginia offensives","detail":"He intended to apply pressure to Confederate armies and resources at the same time rather than allow troops to move freely between theaters."},{"time":"2:05","audioTime":125,"title":"About 165,000 U.S. troops entered Virginia","detail":"Grant and Meade had 122,000 men with the Army of the Potomac; Butler, Sigel, Crook, and Averell led supporting forces elsewhere in the state."},{"time":"4:01","audioTime":241,"title":"Caution weakened the supporting operations","detail":"Several U.S. commanders accepted alarming reports without verification and did not use their numerical advantages, allowing reinforcements to move toward Lee."},{"time":"4:30","audioTime":270,"title":"Grant originally planned one large western raid","detail":"Edward O. C. Ord was to destroy the Virginia and Tennessee Railroad, combine with Crook near Saltville, and, if practical, continue to Lynchburg and the James River."},{"time":"6:02","audioTime":362,"title":"Conflict with Franz Sigel broke up the original plan","detail":"Sigel reduced Ord’s force and Ord asked for reassignment. The operation became three separate columns under Crook, Averell, and Sigel."},{"time":"8:02","audioTime":482,"title":"Crook received the main railroad assignment","detail":"His 6,000 infantry were ordered toward Dublin Depot. Averell’s 2,000 cavalry targeted lead mines and salt works, while Sigel advanced in the Shenandoah Valley."},{"time":"10:02","audioTime":602,"title":"Crook left Gauley Bridge on May 2","detail":"The column contained three infantry brigades and two artillery batteries. Rutherford B. Hayes commanded one of its regiments and strongly supported Crook."},{"time":"12:01","audioTime":721,"title":"Breckinridge’s forces covered 120 miles","detail":"About 7,500 Confederates were scattered across the region. Success depended on learning the U.S. routes early and concentrating faster than the attackers."},{"time":"13:23","audioTime":803,"title":"Breckinridge moved his strongest brigades toward the Shenandoah Valley","detail":"Lee considered Sigel the larger threat. Albert Jenkins remained responsible for the defense against Crook and Averell."},{"time":"16:32","audioTime":992,"title":"Crook turned the New River Narrows position","detail":"The U.S. column crossed Wolf Creek Mountain at Rocky Gap on May 7, placing itself on Jenkins’s flank and forcing the small Confederate force to retreat toward Dublin."},{"time":"17:29","audioTime":1049,"title":"McCausland’s brigade reinforced Jenkins","detail":"The brigade had been waiting for transportation to the Shenandoah Valley at Dublin. Its retention raised Jenkins’s available force to about 3,000."},{"time":"18:00","audioTime":1080,"title":"The armies prepared on opposite sides of Cloyd’s Mountain","detail":"Crook had about 6,500 effective troops at Poplar Hill. Jenkins occupied a high knoll beyond the mountain gap with fence-rail breastworks and nine guns."},{"time":"18:29","audioTime":1109,"title":"Crook observed the full Confederate position","detail":"From near the summit, Crook and Carr White saw the defensive line about two miles away and watched additional Confederate infantry arrive."},{"time":"19:28","audioTime":1168,"title":"Crook planned a frontal attack and a flank movement","detail":"Two brigades advanced directly across the valley while White led the third through dense woods against the Confederate right on Shuffle Ridge."},{"time":"20:17","audioTime":1217,"title":"Jenkins shifted troops toward White","detail":"He first sent a small battalion and then one of his largest regiments to the threatened flank, reducing the strength available against Crook’s main assault."},{"time":"20:28","audioTime":1228,"title":"Sickel and Hayes crossed the meadow","detail":"Horatio Sickel commanded the brigade on the U.S. right and Rutherford B. Hayes the one on the left. Crook accompanied the frontal attack under musket and artillery fire."},{"time":"21:12","audioTime":1272,"title":"Back Creek disrupted the U.S. line","detail":"The four-foot-deep gully was not visible from the mountain. Troops lost formation in the water but used the banks for cover while officers reorganized them."},{"time":"21:35","audioTime":1295,"title":"The U.S. attack captured the breastworks","detail":"Crook’s troops resumed the charge, reached the fence-rail defenses, and overwhelmed the defenders after brief hand-to-hand fighting."},{"time":"21:49","audioTime":1309,"title":"Jenkins was wounded and captured","detail":"He was shot in the arm while trying to rally the line. John McCausland assumed command but could not establish another position."},{"time":"22:05","audioTime":1325,"title":"The Confederate withdrawal became a rout","detail":"Crook’s four hundred cavalrymen increased the pressure. The retreat remained disorganized until the 5th Kentucky arrived near Dublin."},{"time":"22:54","audioTime":1374,"title":"The 5th Kentucky covered the retreat","detail":"Only about four hundred men reached the field after a train derailment. Howard Smith’s regiment held for more than an hour against cavalry and then Hayes’s brigade."},{"time":"23:37","audioTime":1417,"title":"Crook occupied Dublin at dusk","detail":"The episode counts more than one hundred U.S. dead, more than five hundred wounded, and seventy-two missing. Confederate losses included about two hundred missing."},{"time":"24:03","audioTime":1443,"title":"U.S. troops burned railroad facilities on May 10","detail":"They destroyed the Dublin telegraph office, station, and supply depot before moving east to the New River railroad bridge."},{"time":"24:38","audioTime":1478,"title":"Artillery fought over the New River bridge","detail":"After a two-hour exchange, Confederate ammunition ran low and soldiers from Sickel’s brigade set the four-hundred-foot wooden bridge on fire."},{"time":"25:30","audioTime":1530,"title":"Crook withdrew instead of continuing the raid","detail":"McCausland retreated twelve miles toward Christiansburg, but Crook did not pursue. The U.S. column returned through heavy rain and reached Union, West Virginia, on May 14."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Designed the coordinated Virginia operations and assigned the railroad and industrial targets"},{"commander":"George Crook","command":"Kanawha Division","subordinates":"Carr B. White, Horatio G. Sickel, and Hugh Ewing’s brigades"},{"commander":"Carr B. White","command":"1st Brigade","subordinates":"Made the wide movement through woods against the Confederate right"},{"commander":"Horatio G. Sickel","command":"3rd Brigade","subordinates":"Attacked frontally and later burned the New River bridge"},{"commander":"Rutherford B. Hayes","command":"23rd Ohio Infantry","subordinates":"Led troops in the frontal attack and the later assault on the 5th Kentucky"},{"commander":"William W. Averell","command":"Separate cavalry expedition","subordinates":"Assigned to the Wytheville lead mines and Saltville salt works"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"John C. Breckinridge","command":"Department of Western Virginia","subordinates":"Shifted his strongest brigades toward the Shenandoah Valley and left Jenkins in regional command"},{"commander":"Albert G. Jenkins","command":"Cloyd’s Mountain field force","subordinates":"About 3,000 troops including John McCausland’s brigade and nine guns"},{"commander":"John McCausland","command":"Infantry brigade","subordinates":"Took command after Jenkins was wounded and directed the retreat through Dublin"},{"commander":"Howard Smith","command":"5th Kentucky Infantry","subordinates":"About 400 men who formed a rear guard east of Dublin"}]}],"people":[{"name":"George Crook","role":"Planned the successful flank and frontal attacks but ended the wider expedition after destroying the New River bridge","side":"United States","image":"/images/george-crook.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630869/"},{"name":"Carr B. White","role":"Led the brigade that moved through woods and placed the Confederate right under heavy pressure","side":"United States"},{"name":"Rutherford B. Hayes","role":"Commanded the 23rd Ohio and participated in the frontal attack and pursuit","side":"United States"},{"name":"William W. Averell","role":"Led the separate cavalry column assigned to Wytheville and Saltville","side":"United States"},{"name":"Albert G. Jenkins","role":"Formed the Cloyd’s Mountain defense, was wounded during the U.S. breakthrough, and was captured","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/albert-jenkins.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/00650181/"},{"name":"John McCausland","role":"Provided Jenkins’s largest brigade and took command of the retreat after Jenkins fell","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John C. Breckinridge","role":"Oversaw the scattered western Virginia defense while moving troops toward the Shenandoah Valley","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/john-c-breckinridge.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670277/"},{"name":"Howard Smith","role":"Led the 5th Kentucky rear guard that prevented a larger Confederate disaster","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Albert G. Jenkins","side":"Confederacy","role":"Brigadier general commanding at Cloyd’s Mountain","outcome":"Shot in the arm and captured during the collapse of the Confederate line; the wound required amputation and he died later in May 1864"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/cloyds-mountain-tactical-map.svg","alt":"Schematic of the attack at Cloyd’s Mountain","caption":"Crook fixed the Confederate center with two brigades while White moved through woods against the right.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va049"},{"image":"/images/george-crook.jpg","alt":"Civil War portrait of George Crook","caption":"Crook led the frontal attack personally and used his numerical advantage to carry the Confederate breastworks.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630869/"},{"image":"/images/albert-jenkins.jpg","alt":"Portrait engraving of Albert G. Jenkins","caption":"Jenkins assembled about 3,000 men, held until the U.S. attack reached his works, and was mortally wounded and captured.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/00650181/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 487","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/03/23/civil-war-podcast-episode-487/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Cloyd’s Mountain battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va049"},{"label":"Virginia Tech: Battle of Cloyd’s Mountain","url":"https://civilwar.vt.edu/programs/drivingtour/battleofcloydsmountain.html"},{"label":"Library of Congress: George Crook portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630869/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Albert G. Jenkins portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/00650181/"}]}}]}]
